  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 215 AM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Surface high pressure is building into the region today, \nwhich will maintain a slightly cooler than normal air mass with \nabundant dry air aloft. The low humidity pattern will flip beginning \nSunday afternoon, and especially evening, as this high exits to our \neast and increasing moisture transport from the southwest occurs \nahead of an occluding area of low pressure moving into the eastern \nGreat Lakes. \n\nIt continues to appear that weakly unstable air aloft will \nsupport moderate rainfall rates (as high as an inch/hour), and a\nfew rumbles of thunder at times. The heavier showers should \ntend to be at the nose of a strengthening nocturnal low level \njet (850 millibar winds increasing to around 30 knots or so). \nThe placement of the heavier rain is rather uncertain at this \ntime and will tend to be wherever a north-south moisture \ngradient sets up overnight. The latest guidance shows a broad \nconsensus in portions of northern/northeastern Vermont, but \ngiven large spread in recent forecast cycles of where heavier \nfootprints of rain will fall we have maintained a blend showing \nroughly 0.5\" of rain in much of our region. The event total 75th\npercentile looks like reasonable higher end rainfall amounts, \nshowing potential for swaths of rainfall in the 1-1.5\" range in \nVermont with less potential in the Adirondacks and points west. \nIf heavier rain occurs farther south, the southwesterly low \nlevel flow will support locally heavier totals in the vicinity \nof the Green Mountain National Forest. Antecedent dry \nconditions and lack of a stationary boundary or setup for \ntraining showers are limiting factors for flash flood risk, such\nthat a marginal (level 1) excessive rainfall outlook looks \nreasonable for our region. The steady rain will exit to our east\ntowards Monday morning with the departure of isentropic lift \nand a broad area of surface low pressure into the region.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A deeper area of low pressure over northern Quebec will \nbe dragging a weak cold front through the area during the day on \nMonday. In the wake of the early morning rain, there will be plenty \nof shallow moisture/elevated dew points such to support scattered to \nnumerous showers during the day ahead of this front. Any modest \nheating will increase instability, although shallow cloud tops are \nexpected due to a wedge of warm, dry air aloft and limit downpour \nintensity with low chances for thunder. As drier air continues to \nfilter through on deepening west-northwesterly flow, rain chances \nwill become orographically forced with otherwise increasing sunshine \nthrough the later part of the day.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Pretty much remain in a broad cyclonic flow that is \ndominating the Great Lakes-NY-New England. Heat ridge remains mainly \nacross southern-southwest tier of CONUS. Our CWA is far enough from \nthe periphery of the ridge that we stay largely influenced by \nthe northern stream flow with seasonable temperatures and a \nchance of showers now and then. The best chance for showers will\nbe late Wed- Thu with a northern stream system.",
